@startuml dataflow_example_multi_var
' Worked example: notebooks/tutorial3.ipynb, case "overlap_le1_1d".
' Two variables on one 3x3 grid, the second varying along one dimension.
' Every number here is from an actual run of that case.
' Grids: rows x0, columns x1, in index space.
' Render: plantuml docs/dataflow_example_multi_var.puml

title Example 2 -- two variables, one of them 1D (tutorial3, "overlap_le1_1d")

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FCFCFC
  BorderColor #777777
  DiamondBackgroundColor #F2F2F2
}
skinparam partition {
  BorderColor #8899AA
  FontColor #33495E
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFDF0
  BorderColor #C8C08A
}

start

partition "call" {
  :GenerateData(num_obs=8, num_dims=2, ratio_dims=1,
                density=[8/9, 8/9], seed=12345,
                num_vars=2, var_dims=[2, 1],
                overlap=1/8, fixed_overlap=False)
  .generate(...);
  note right
    var_dims=[2,1]: var0 varies along both dimensions,
    var1 along one. The GRID stays 2D either way.
    overlap is the share of <b>var0's</b> sites that
    also carry var1, on the dimensions the two share.
  end note
}

partition "validate and configure" {
  :shape = [3, 3], 9 sites, density_zero = 3/9 = 0.333;

  :<b>MultiVarDimensionsConfig</b>  streams VAR_DIMS, CONST_COORD
  var0 varies along x0 and x1
  var1 varies along x1 only, constant along x0;

  :<b>MultiVarSparsityConfig</b>
  a density is a share of the variable's OWN grid:
  var0: 8/9 of 9 cells = 8
  var1: 8/9 of 3 cells = 2.67 -> rint -> 3
  -> var_num_obs = 8, 3;
  note right
    var1 fills its whole axis: 3 of 3 cells.
  end note

  :<b>dim_split = 1</b>, not 0
  the split axis must be one EVERY variable varies along,
  and var1 is constant along x0;
  note right
    printed: "Stratifying along dimension 1 (size 3)
    rather than the largest dimension 0 (size 3),
    which is not shared by every variable."
    The strata are the COLUMNS x1 = 0, 1, 2 here.
  end note
}

partition "coordinates" {
  :the same two streams as Example 1, so the axes
  depend on the seed and the shape and nothing else:
  x0 = 0.1809, 0.3500, 0.6837
  x1 = 0.7887, 0.9100, 0.9353;
}

partition "placement of var0: LHS, then per-stratum fill" {
  :<b>LHS stage</b>, stream(12345, LHS), n_s = 3
  the same draw as Example 1: sites (0,1), (1,2), (2,0);
  note right
    after the LHS stage
    (L = LHS site, . = vacant)
            x1=0 x1=1 x1=2
      x0=0   .    L    .
      x0=1   .    .    L
      x0=2   L    .    .
  end note

  :<b>fill counts</b>
  8 - 3 = 5 fill points over 3 columns,
  2 free cells each -> fill per stratum = 1, 2, 2;

  :each stratum j draws its fill sites, then its values,
  from stream(12345, STRATUM, j)
  stratum 0: fill (0,0)
  stratum 1: fill (2,1), (1,1)
  stratum 2: fill (0,2), (2,2);
  note right
    after the fill
    (f = fill site)
            x1=0 x1=1 x1=2
      x0=0   f    L    f
      x0=1   .    f    L
      x0=2   L    f    f
    var0 per stratum: p = 2, 3, 3 sites
  end note
}

partition "placement of var1, one stratum at a time" {
  :<b>pinned coordinate</b>: _select_constant_coords
  with stream(12345, CONST_COORD, 1, 0) -> <b>x0 = 2</b>;

  :<b>counts</b>: var1 varies along the split axis only, so
  each stratum offers it 1 cell
  apportion(3, weights 1, 1, 1) -> 1, 1, 1;

  :<b>per stratum j</b>, rng = stream(12345, VAR, 1, j)
  proj_j(S0) = var0's sites seen through var1's dims
  other than the split: none, so 1 cell
  free cells = 1 - 1 = 0
  ideal = round(0.125 * 1) = 0
  lo = 1 - 0 = 1 (nowhere else to go), hi = 1
  -> n_overlap = 1 in every stratum;
  note right #FDF1F1
    printed: "WARNING var1: overlap target not reachable
    in 3 of 3 strata (e.g. wanted 0 of 1 reference cells,
    used 1; 0 cells free of var0). Density takes precedence."
    The LHS stage puts var0 in every column, so var0's
    projection onto x1 is the whole axis and no cell is
    free of var0. With var1 on every cell of x1, the
    pair (density, overlap) is over-determined.
  end note

  :values from the same VAR stream
  (2,0) = 0.842, (2,1) = 0.328, (2,2) = 0.759;
  note right
    var1 placed
    (0 = var0 only, B = both, . = vacant)
            x1=0 x1=1 x1=2
      x0=0   0    0    0
      x0=1   .    0    0
      x0=2   B    B    B
    var1 sits on row x0 = 2, its pinned coordinate.
  end note

  :<b>OverlapCalculator.compute_overlap_report</b>
  on x1, var0 uses all 3 coordinates and var1 all 3
  <b>overlap = 1.000</b> (target was 0.125)
  <b>overlap_r = 1.000</b>;
  note right
    printed: "Minimum feasible overlap per non-reference
    variable given grid points and observations: [0.0]"
    compute_min_overlap reports 0.0 for any variable on
    fewer dimensions and leaves it to the per-stratum
    check above; the achievable minimum here is 1.
  end note
}

partition "two in-memory stores, which differ in shape here" {
  :<b>NetCDFBuilder.build_dataset</b>
  var0 (x0, x1) 3x3, then squeeze_constant_dims drops x0
  from var1, leaving <b>var1 (x1)</b> = 0.842, 0.328, 0.759
  attrs add num_vars=2, var_densities, var_num_obs=[8, 3],
  overlap_target=[0.125], overlap_convention,
  fixed_overlap=[0], overlap_actual=[1.0],
  overlap_actual_reverse=[1.0];
  note right
    Squeezing is deliberate: a variable pinned to one
    coordinate carries no information along that axis,
    and a NaN slab would inflate the array side, which
    is the thing being measured.
    The parquet drops it too: var1's value holds at every
    x0, so it repeats on each row with its x1.
  end note

  :<b>ParquetBuilder.build_multi_var_dataframe</b>
  one row per var0 site (8), one column per variable,
  x1 (= dim_split) slowest; var1 (x1 only) repeats on
  every row with its x1;
  note right
    |= x0 |= x1 |= var0 |= var1 |
    | 0.180929 | 0.788706 | 0.320146 | 0.841526 |
    | 0.683725 | 0.788706 | 0.550290 | 0.841526 |
    | 0.180929 | 0.910005 | 0.742040 | 0.327880 |
    | 0.349984 | 0.910005 | 0.802223 | 0.327880 |
    | 0.683725 | 0.910005 | 0.023239 | 0.327880 |
    | 0.180929 | 0.935274 | 0.134245 | 0.758960 |
    | 0.349984 | 0.935274 | 0.547495 | 0.758960 |
    | 0.683725 | 0.935274 | 0.868300 | 0.758960 |
    A variable absent at a coordinate costs one NaN cell
    per row here, and a whole grid slot on the array side.
  end note
}

partition "disk" {
  :netCDF/ds.nc                13243 B
  parquet/ddf_0.parquet         3888 B
  parquet/_metadata             3326 B
  parquet/_common_metadata      2704 B;
}

:return (Dataset, DataFrame);
stop

@enduml
